Use the AutofillClient boundary for embedder network policy. No existing
crowdsourcing-specific consent/policy method exists on this base client.
The new default-true method preserves other embedders and flag-absent Chrome.
Only Chrome reads chrome/common/chrome_switches.h; components remain independent
of Chrome. The switch is inline constexpr in //chrome/common:non_code_constants.

With --disable-background-networking, IsEnabled() refuses both automatic server
prediction queries and crowdsourcing vote uploads before encoding, cache lookup,
upload throttling, or URL loader creation. This includes Password Manager votes
using the same crowdsourcing manager. This is not credit-card/Wallet upload,
page requests, authentication, cookies, TLS, Safe Browsing, security component
data, or explicit model demand. Local heuristics, Autofill preferences, form
filling, DOM editing, and native input paths are not disabled or modified.

Queries keep the existing failure contract: return false and synchronously call
a supplied callback exactly once with std::nullopt. No synthetic prediction
response is generated. Uploads return false, have no callback, and do not consume
upload-throttling state. No request means no network completion/error or retry.
The process switch is a startup policy; this patch does not cancel an already
in-flight request if tests mutate the command line later.

Added regressions (not executed):
- AutofillCrowdsourcingManagerTest.ClientCanDisableCrowdsourcing: valid form and
  encoded votes produce no loaders under denied policy, query callback receives
  std::nullopt once, both Autofill and Password Manager votes are refused, and
  the same query/vote produce two loaders after allowing policy. Default client
  policy and local Autofill/profile/Autocomplete enablement remain true.
- ChromeAutofillClientTest.BackgroundNetworkingOnlyDisablesCrowdsourcing:
  flag-absent/flag-present/flag-removed policy and manager enablement, with local
  Autofill/profile/Autocomplete enablement unchanged while the flag is present.

Candidate only. No build, test, lint, formatter, or applicability check ran.
Parent must apply against the exact base, compile component and Chrome unit
tests, run both new regressions and existing related suites, then prove G24
network and native-input behavior with the rebuilt browser before pinning.

